<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valencia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valencia is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, surrounded by a fertile irrigated plain known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huerta">huerta</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it was an important city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. The Almoravid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> profiles cite the irrigated huertas of Valencia among the most productive agricultural zones of their Iberian territories.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valencia.jpg" alt="Valencia" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boris Dzhingarov · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malvarrosa_Beach,_Valencia,_Spain_(29812271043).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Valencia lies on the eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, fronting the Gulf of Valencia, on the highly fertile alluvial silts of the floodplain formed in the lower course of the Turia River. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> founded it in 138 BCE it stood on the alluvial plain several kilometres from the sea. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name was Valentia, meaning strength or valour, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> says the colony was founded by Roman soldiers who had fought the Lusitanian rebel Viriatus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Founded around 138 BCE as Valentia Edetanorum, Valencia is one of the oldest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. It later became part of the Visigothic Kingdom and surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> around 714. In the wake of the fitna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> it became the head of the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of Valencia, which saw notable urban development. The Jewish poet and philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> ibn Gabirol spent his last years there.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> fell in 1085 the territory became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a>. A revolt in 1092 handed the city to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>, and in 1094 El Cid took it by force and established his own principality. With the Aragonese Christian conquest of 1238 Valencia became the capital of the Kingdom of Valencia. Its trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> and Italian ports made it thrive in the 15th century, and by the end of that century it had become one of the largest European cities. The University of Valencia was founded in 1499.</p><p>Between 1521 and 1522 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-brotherhoods">Revolt of the Brotherhoods</a> plunged the kingdom into civil war, and Muslim vassals were forced to convert in 1526 at the behest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. Its economy suffered a crisis after the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a> in 1609. The city became a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>-manufacturing centre in the 18th century. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> it was the provisional seat of the Spanish Government from 1936 to 1937.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Valencia is the capital and largest city of the Valencian Community and of the province of the same name, and the third most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Valencian and Spanish are the official languages. Its historic centre is among the largest in Spain, and its monuments include Valencia Cathedral, the Torres de Serrans, the Torres de Quart and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llotja-de-la-seda">Lonja de la Seda</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1996. The Falles festival, held in March, was recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as intangible cultural heritage in 2016.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Valencia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The absorption of its remaining independent Muslim principalities, the last being the kingdom of Ibn Mardanish in Murcia and <b>Valencia</b>, was completed under his son Abu Yaqub Yusuf I in 1172.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Andalusian irrigated agriculture, the huertas of <b>Valencia</b>, Murcia and Granada, drew on a hydraulic tradition that built on Roman foundations and techniques introduced from the Islamic East, and it produced surpluses that supported dense urban populations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The caliphal title passed between Umayyads and the Hammudids, a Berber-backed dynasty of Idrisid descent, while governors, Saqaliba commanders and local notables seized power in cities such as Seville, Granada, Zaragoza, <b>Valencia</b> and Toledo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His son James I the Conqueror turned south and seaward, conquering Majorca from 1229 and <b>Valencia</b>, whose capital surrendered in 1238.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Because Aragon, <b>Valencia</b> and Catalonia had largely backed the Habsburg claimant, Philip abolished their separate institutions by the Nueva Planta decrees of 1707–1716 and imposed Castilian law and administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The new state stretched along the mountains and coast of the south-east, from the region of Gibraltar to beyond Almería, shielded by the Sierra Nevada, and it received waves of refugees from Seville, Córdoba, <b>Valencia</b> and Murcia, becoming the last refuge of Andalusi Islam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
